# Appendix I — Glossary

This glossary gives plain-language meanings for terms used throughout the book. A
term's linked chapter provides the fuller model, invariants, and limitations. Syntax
forms and settings are collected separately in
[Appendix B — Literate syntax and settings reference](appendix_syntax_settings.html).

```TERM adapter "Adapter"
Code that connects the shared semantic engine to a particular environment or effect.

The CLI adapter reads disk and reports to a terminal; the editor adapter reads
workspace snapshots and uses VS Code APIs. An adapter may write a validated plan, but
it does not define a second fragment language. See
[One generator, several hosts](one_generator_several_hosts.html).
```

## Annotation

Renderer-neutral explanatory material attached to the book. Literate annotations
include reusable term definitions, explicit term uses, and numbered notes. Screen and
print render them differently while sharing identity and resolution. See
[Shared annotations and identities](shared_annotations_identities.html).

## Append

An authored contribution declared with `=+` after a family's base definition. Appends
join the family in project encounter order. They are not text patched into a generated
file after tangling; they are ordered source contributions known before expansion.
See [Choosing fragments well](choosing_fragments.html).

## Assembly map

A mechanical reference showing how root and gathering fragments compose complete
generated files. It answers “which pieces make this output?” while conceptual
chapters explain why those pieces exist. See
[Appendix A — Program assembly maps](assembly.html).

## Bootstrap

Known-working material used to build the generator before a freshly generated version
exists. In this repository, checked-in generated source and a buildable CLI provide
the bridge from the authoritative book to a fresh engine. Bootstrap files are useful
derived artifacts, not a second authority. See
[A generator that generates itself](generator_that_generates_itself.html).

## Capability

A narrowly scoped authority represented by data that must be validated before use.
Examples include an opaque preview-navigation URI, an allowed executable path, or a
resource path confined to a project. Possessing arbitrary text that resembles a
capability does not grant the authority. See
[Previewing the explanation](previewing_the_explanation.html).

## Captured input

The exact documents and templates selected for one operation. Capturing turns a
changing workspace into a stable value that can be parsed, planned, checked for
freshness, or sent to another process. Later edits do not retroactively change that
operation. See [The project snapshot](project_snapshot.html).

## Companion

A file that travels beside a LaTeX publication, such as an image, diagram, font, or
font license. Companions use safe generated names and join the same plan and
transaction as the TeX document. See
[Portable publication resources](portable_publication_resources.html).

```TERM contribution "Contribution"
One authored fenced block belonging to a named fragment family.

The first accepted contribution is the definition; later accepted `=+` blocks are
appends. Contributions retain separate source locations even though tangling
concatenates them. See [The fragment graph](fragment_graph.html).
```

## Definition

The first accepted `=` declaration of a fragment family. It establishes the exact,
case-sensitive family name and, for a root, its output destination and optional
source template. A second base definition of the same name is a duplicate, not an
append. See [From explanation to source](from_explanation_to_source.html).

```TERM determinism "Determinism"
The property of producing the same semantic result from the same accepted input and
policy.

In Literate this includes document order, contribution order, graph traversal,
identities, diagnostics, and planned output bytes. Determinism does not mean that
identities survive arbitrary edits or that external PDF tools produce identical
bytes on every machine. See
[Determinism as a design principle](determinism_design_principle.html).
```

## Diagnostic

A structured explanation that an input, graph, mapping, resource, or operation is
invalid or unsupported. Engine diagnostics name a source path and zero-based line and
column. A diagnostic prevents an all-or-nothing plan where continuing would require
guessing or publishing partial results.

## Effect

An observable interaction outside pure parsing and planning: reading a filesystem,
writing an output, spawning a process, registering an editor provider, or opening a
UI. Literate keeps effects in adapters so the same semantic model can serve several
hosts. See [Parsing without side effects](parsing_without_effects.html).

```TERM fail-closed "Fail closed"
Refusing an operation when its safety or meaning cannot be established.

A missing mapping, malformed protocol reply, excessive payload, ambiguous term, or
partial code action produces no invented fallback. Read-only views may omit unsafe
data; mutating operations reject the complete change. See
[Resource bounds and fail-closed behavior](resource_bounds_fail_closed.html).
```

```TERM fragment-family "Fragment family"
All ordered contributions with one exact fragment name.

References point to the family, not to one contribution. Tangling expands the family
by concatenating its base definition and appends in encounter order. See
[The fragment graph](fragment_graph.html).
```

```TERM fixed-point "Fixed point"
A state in which a fresh engine built from the generated plan reads the same captured
book and reproduces exactly the same output path set and contents.

A fixed point shows self-consistency of generation; it does not prove all application
behavior correct. See
[A generator that generates itself](generator_that_generates_itself.html).
```

```TERM fragment "Fragment"
A named unit of authored program text chosen to express one explainable idea.

In casual prose, “fragment” may refer to the family as a whole; when precision
matters, a family contains one or more contributions. References let fragments be
presented in explanatory order and assembled in source order. See
[Choosing fragments well](choosing_fragments.html).
```

## Fragment graph

The directed graph whose vertices are fragment families and whose edges are
references from one family to another. Roots select generated outputs. Missing
vertices and cycles are graph errors. See [The fragment graph](fragment_graph.html).

```TERM freshness "Freshness"
Evidence that a result still belongs to the source, templates, settings, workspace,
session, and request that produced it.

Version numbers can prove some forms of freshness; unversioned asynchronous reports
may only offer best-effort freshness. Known stale work is discarded rather than
attached to current source. See
[Diagnostics over changing projections](diagnostics_over_projections.html).
```

## Generated output

A file described by a complete generation or publication plan. Generated program
source and HTML are derived from the authoritative book; publication outputs are
derived through the separate explicit pipeline. Generated files are important review,
bootstrap, and packaging artifacts, but direct edits do not become authoritative.

```TERM generation-plan "Generation plan"
The complete set of validated output paths and contents returned before a host writes
anything.

Planning is all-or-nothing: graph, template, destination, collision, and resource
errors produce diagnostics and no partial file set. See
[Planning a complete generation](complete_generation_plan.html).
```

```TERM host "Host"
The environment that supplies inputs and owns effects around the shared engine.

Literate's hosts include the command line, VS Code, Markdown preview, browser programs,
and publication helpers. A host may have narrower trust, filesystem, or process
capabilities than another host.
```

## Identity

A stable target derived from semantic data such as an exact fragment name, canonical
document path, heading slug, term key, or source location. Separate namespaces keep
families, contributions, documents, headings, terms, and notes from colliding.
Identity is deterministic under fixed input but may change after a meaningful rename
or move. See [Building a publication document](building_publication_document.html).

## Inspection

A read-only report of ordered documents, template dependencies, fragment declarations,
roots, and references. Inspection can retain useful partial structure from an invalid
project and does not read or preflight generated destinations. See
[The command-line adapter](command_line_adapter.html).

## Language server

A separate program that provides language intelligence through the Language Server
Protocol. Literate launches explicitly configured stdio servers for generated-language
projections and validates their replies before mapping results into fragments. It does
not borrow another extension's private server or install one automatically. See
[Owning a language-server session](owning_language_server_session.html).

## Mapping segment

A half-open correspondence between unchanged literal text in a literate contribution
and one interval in assembled generated text. Inserted indentation, reference markers,
template wrappers, and other synthetic text have no source mapping. See
[The projection problem](projection_problem.html).

```TERM occurrence "Occurrence"
One appearance of source text in a generated projection.

A reused fragment can have several occurrences. Read-only services can select a
deterministic occurrence; mutating services require a uniquely safe occurrence so one
edit is not unexpectedly amplified through every expansion. See
[Mutating projected source safely](mutating_projected_source.html).
```

## Output root

A fragment family whose name ends in `.*` and whose initial declaration names a
workspace-relative destination. Expanding a root produces one generated source file,
possibly through a source template. “Root” may also mean the workspace directory in
filesystem discussions; the surrounding phrase should make the meaning clear. See
[From explanation to source](from_explanation_to_source.html).

```TERM output-transaction "Output transaction"
The host protocol for safely publishing all changed files in a complete validated
plan.

It compares planned bytes, preflights every destination, stages all changed files,
preserves originals, installs the batch, rolls back ordinary failures, and reports
retained recovery material. It offers recoverable exception safety, not a
crash-durable journal, concurrency lock, or instant filesystem-wide atomicity. See
[Changing files safely](changing_files_safely.html).
```

## Pandoc bridge

The controlled conversion boundary from Literate's validated publication model to a
small Pandoc JSON document and then to a LaTeX body. Pandoc is a trusted prose writer
inside this boundary, not the parser of Literate source or the owner of fragment
semantics. See [Building a publication document](building_publication_document.html).

## Plan

A complete proposed result returned before effects. Depending on context, this may be
a generation plan, resource plan, or publication plan. A plan makes validation and
review possible before a transaction changes destinations.

## Preview

A screen presentation intended for reading while editing. Markdown preview can use
current buffers, semantic links, Mermaid rendering, and optional fragment exploration,
but it does not define generation authority or imply that print publication will have
the same layout. See [Previewing the explanation](previewing_the_explanation.html).

## Project

The semantic unit formed from every eligible `.literate` document under one root,
plus declared templates and root settings. The root index controls the beginning of
document order but does not exclude unlinked chapters. See
[The project snapshot](project_snapshot.html).

```TERM projection "Projection"
An in-memory view of assembled generated-language files presented at their intended
URIs without requiring those files to be written.

A projection includes source mappings so validated language-server results can return
to authored fragments. See [The projection problem](projection_problem.html).
```

## Protocol

An explicit contract for exchanging data between independently owned components.
Examples include JSON-RPC framing for language servers, inert browser payloads, the
publication helper's single JSON request, and versioned CLI responses. A protocol
includes validation, limits, lifecycle, and failure rules—not only a JSON shape.

## Publication document

The complete renderer-neutral block tree produced after project order, fragment graph,
links, annotations, and supported Markdown have all been validated. Screen and print
renderers consume related semantic data, while the Pandoc bridge carries the print
document toward LaTeX. See
[Building a publication document](building_publication_document.html).

```TERM reference "Reference"
A `<<name>>` use inside an accepted contribution that tangling replaces recursively
with the target family's ordered contributions.

Its use-site indentation is applied to every expanded line. An escaped opening
delimiter is literal text rather than a reference. See
[From explanation to source](from_explanation_to_source.html).
```

## Renderer

A component that turns an already understood model into presentation for a medium.
HTML, editor preview, LaTeX, syntax highlighting, annotation rails, and Mermaid assets
have renderers. Renderers decide appearance but do not redefine fragment identity or
project order.

## Root settings

Project-wide options authored in exact `SETTINGS` fences in normalized root
`index.literate`. Source/HTML settings select template, authors, and theme; print
publication additionally reads font roles, bounded sizes, and code-ligature policy. Chapter-local
settings do not override the root. See
[Appendix B — Literate syntax and settings reference](appendix_syntax_settings.html).

## Self-hosting

Using Literate to describe and generate Literate's own engine. Self-hosting creates a
bootstrap obligation: an available generator must understand enough of the book to
produce its replacement. It is verified by a fixed point rather than assumed from the
circular description. See
[A generator that generates itself](generator_that_generates_itself.html).

```TERM project-snapshot "Project snapshot"
An ordered, immutable capture of project inputs for one operation.

In the CLI it is built from saved disk files. In the editor, eligible open buffers can
override disk, including empty or newly created file-backed documents. A snapshot is
not a live workspace view. See [The project snapshot](project_snapshot.html).
```

```TERM source-mapping "Source mapping"
The collection of mapping segments that relates authored fragment text to generated
projection text.

Mappings let diagnostics, navigation, completion ranges, and selected edits return to
source without searching by text. A missing or seam-crossing mapping is omitted rather
than guessed.
```

## Stable target

A collision-safe identifier used for semantic links. Fragment-family targets derive
from exact names; contribution targets additionally use canonical path and local
ordinal; document, heading, term, and note targets use their own namespaces. Stable
means deterministic for the same structure, not permanent across every edit.

## Tangle

To expand roots and named references into generated program source. Tangling follows
the fragment graph, concatenates contributions in project order, preserves use-site
indentation, applies source templates, and reports graph errors. In this book,
“generate” includes tangling plus HTML planning and host-side output handling. See
[From explanation to source](from_explanation_to_source.html).

## Template

A captured input containing a fixed literal placeholder. Source templates replace
every original `[CODE]`; HTML templates replace `[CONTENT]`, `[AUTHORS]`, and
`[THEME]`. Substitution is single-pass, and inserted values are not scanned again.
Template wrapper text has no authored fragment mapping.

## Term

A named reusable definition authored with a `TERM` fence and referenced only through
explicit `term:` links. A term definition remains in prose; generated indexes and
print page lookups point to it rather than relocating its body. See
[Shared annotations and identities](shared_annotations_identities.html).

## Trust boundary

A point where data or authority must be checked before crossing into a more powerful
operation. Examples include accepting a language-server reply, selecting an
executable, resolving an output path, turning author structure into raw LaTeX, or
committing planned bytes. A trust boundary narrows authority; it is not automatically
a sandbox.

## Weave

To render the explanatory work for people, traditionally as a document with prose,
code, and cross-references. Literate's screen weaving appears as generated HTML and
Markdown preview; print publication uses a separate validated path through Pandoc and
LaTeX. The implementation often says “render” or “publish” for these concrete forms.
See [Programs for people and machines](programs_for_people_and_machines.html).

## Workspace root

The directory that bounds one project's discovery, relative paths, templates, inputs,
and outputs. Paths are validated against this authority. This filesystem meaning is
distinct from an output-root fragment.
